1. e4 c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Nf3 g6 4. g3 {Slightly unusual with the knight committed to f3:} (4. d4 {takes play into more normal lines.}) 4... Bg7 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O e6 (6... Bg4 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 e6 9. Ne2 Nge7 10. Qb3 O-O 11. c3 Qd7 12. d3 d5 13. Be3 b6 14. Rad1 d4 15. Bc1 Rab8 16. f4 b5 17. g4 dxc3 18. bxc3 b4 19. e5 Rfd8 20. Qc4 Nd5 21. cxb4 {Romanishin,O (2595)-Gulko,B (2585)/Leningrad 1977/URS-ch/0-1 (44)}) (6... e5 7. Nd5 Nge7 8. Nxe7 Qxe7 9. c3 O-O 10. d3 b5 11. Bg5 Qc7 12. Qd2 b4 13. d4 bxc3 14. bxc3 exd4 15. cxd4 Nxd4 16. Nxd4 Bxd4 17. Rac1 Qd7 18. e5 Ba6 19. Rfe1 dxe5 20. Rxc5 Bb7 21. Bf6 {Kretzschmann,J (2200)-Schweitzer,A (2221)/Germany 2006/1/2-1/2 (36)}) 7. Ne2 Nge7 8. d4 cxd4 (8... O-O 9. c3 b6 10. Be3 Bb7 11. Nh4 cxd4 12. cxd4 Rc8 13. Qd2 Ba6 14. Rfd1 h6 15. f4 d5 16. e5 Nf5 17. Nxf5 gxf5 18. Nc3 Qe7 19. g4 fxg4 20. f5 exf5 21. Bxh6 Bxh6 22. Qxh6 Qe6 23. Qg5+ {Bielinski,L (2245)-Chojnicki,Z (2245)/Lubniewice 1998/1-0 (44)}) 9. Nexd4 {Diagram # A somewhat unusual Dragon-style position has been reached; the d6 pawn is slightly weak, but black has developed slightly more quickly than normal. Chances are finely balanced.} O-O 10. Be3 a6 11. a4 Qc7 12. c3 Na5 {A reasonable idea, with the knight able to strike at c4, but it later ends up being misplaced.} (12... Bd7 {is also playable, just developing a piece and seeing what white does.}) 13. Nd2 Bd7 14. Qe2 Rac8 15. Rfc1 f5 (15... e5 16. Nc2 f5 {could also be considered.}) 16. exf5 gxf5 $4 {Diagram #} (16... Nxf5 $15) 17. Bg5 $1 Bxd4 $2 (17... Kf7 18. Re1 Bxd4 19. cxd4 {leaves white with the two bishops and black's king totally exposed, but this would have been better than the game.}) 18. cxd4 Qd8 19. d5 $1 {An unusual sort of double attack, after which either the e-pawn or the a5 knight must go.} e5 20. b4 Rf7 21. Rxc8 Qxc8 22. Bxe7 {Perhaps a little premature; this exchange can be made at any time, so white should wait for the best moment.} Rxe7 23. bxa5 Qc3 24. Ra2 Qxa5 25. Nc4 Qc5 26. Qc2 Qb4 $6 {Black is lost anyway, but this makes matters worse - the white queen can now invade along the c1-h6 diagonal.} (26... Rg7 {is slightly tougher.}) 27. Qc1 b5 28. axb5 Bxb5 (28... axb5 29. Qg5+ Kf7 30. Ra8 Re8 31. Ra7 Re7 32. Bf3 {is terminal.}) 29. Qg5+ Kf7 30. Qxf5+ Kg7 31. Qg4+ Kf8 32. Bf1 e4 33. Qf4+ Kg8 {Diagram #} 34. Qxd6 (34. Nxd6 {wins more quickly, although the text is perfectly adequate:} Bxf1 35. Qg5+ Rg7 36. Qd8# {is the key line.}) 34... Qxd6 35. Nxd6 Bxf1 36. Kxf1 Rd7 37. Rxa6 1-0

