1. e4 e5 2. d4 {The Centre Game was a rare guest in tournaments until 1994, when Morozevich famously used it to beat Mark Hebden at the Lloyds Bank Masters. Since then, while never exactly being popular, it has surfaced a number of times in tournaments.} exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 4. Qe3 Nf6 5. Bd2 Bb4 6. Nc3 O-O 7. O-O-O Re8 8. Qg3 $5 {Diagram # This gambit is what revived the line; white gives up a central pawn for development, attacking chances, and, in a number of lines, the two bishops.} Rxe4 {Black takes on the challenge.} (8... d6 9. f4 Nxe4 10. Nxe4 Rxe4 11. c3 Bc5 12. Bd3 Re8 13. Nf3 h6 (13... Qf6 14. Ng5 Bf5 15. Bc4 Bg6 16. Qg4 Na5 17. Bb5 c6 18. Bf1 b5 19. Qh3 Nc4 20. Bxc4 bxc4 21. Rhe1 h6 22. Rxe8+ Rxe8 23. Nf3 Rb8 24. Qh4 Ba3 {0-1 Wlodarski,M (1914)-Kucharski,K (1800)/Chojnice 2006}) 14. Rde1 Rxe1+ 15. Rxe1 Bd7 16. h3 Qf6 17. Qh2 Bf5 18. Bf1 Be6 19. g4 Bxa2 20. Bd3 Na5 21. Qe2 Qe6 22. Qf1 Nb3+ 23. Kc2 {Mrdjen,D (2095)-Krstic,U (2270)/Pula 1997/0-1 (52)}) (8... Ne5 9. Re1 d6 10. h4 Be6 11. h5 Kh8 12. h6 gxh6 13. Bxh6 Rg8 14. Bg5 Bxc3 15. bxc3 c5 16. f4 Rxg5 17. Qxg5 Ned7 18. f5 Bxa2 19. Re3 Qa5 20. Rg3 {1-0 Vukovic,Z (2470)-Carnic,S (2320)/Nis 1995}) 9. a3 (9. Nxe4 $4 Nxe4 $19) 9... Bxc3 (9... Ba5 $5 {is also worthy of consideration.}) 10. Bxc3 d6 11. Bd3 Rg4 12. Qf3 Rg5 13. Qe3 Ng4 (13... Nd5 {could also be played here:} 14. Bxh7+ Kf8 15. Rxd5 Rxd5 {leads to another position where white is material down but with compensation.}) 14. Qd2 (14. Qf4 Nge5 15. Bxh7+ Kxh7 16. Qh4+ Kg8 17. f4 Rxg2 18. Qxd8+ Nxd8 19. fxe5 Bf5 20. Re1 dxe5 21. Rxe5 Rxc2+ 22. Kd1 Bg6 23. Re8+ Kh7 24. h4 f6 25. Rf8 Rg2 26. Nh3 Rb8 27. Ng5+ Kh6 28. Rh8+ Bh7 {1-0 Lambertz,W (1914)-Bock,A (2106)/Bergheim 2003}) 14... Nge5 15. Bf1 Ng6 16. f4 (16. h4 {is another idea, attempting to dislodge the knight from its fine defensive square.} Rh5 {seems to leave white without a consistent follow-up, though.}) 16... Rc5 17. g3 a5 18. Bg2 Bd7 19. Nf3 b5 {Diagram #} 20. b4 $2 {Over the last few moves, black has consolidated his extra pawn and started an attack of his own. The text gives white a temporary material advantage, but opens up his king too much.} axb4 21. axb4 Rxc3 22. Qxc3 Ra4 23. Kd2 Nxb4 24. Ra1 Nd5 25. Qb3 (25. Qd3 Ngxf4 26. gxf4 Nxf4 27. Qf1 Rxa1) 25... Nb6 26. Rxa4 bxa4 27. Qc3 d5 (27... Ne7 {is another move, trying to get the knight into d5 while leaving the other knight able to menace c4.}) 28. Ke2 Nc4 29. Nd2 Qe7+ 30. Kf3 $2 {Diagram #} (30. Kf2 {is the only move:}) (30. Kf1 Ne3+ 31. Kf2 (31. Kg1 Nd1 $19) 31... Ng4+ 32. Kf3 c5 33. Qa3 Bf5 $19 34. Rb1 Be4+ 35. Kxg4 Qe6+) (30. Kd1 Ne3+ 31. Kc1 Nxg2 $19) 30... Nxd2+ $2 (30... Bg4+ $3 {wins for black:} 31. Kxg4 Qe2+ 32. Bf3 (32. Nf3 Qe6+ 33. Kh5 f5 {(threatening ...Nxf4+ and ...Qh6#)} 34. g4 Nxf4+ 35. Kh4 (35. Kg5 Qg6+ 36. Kxf4 Qxg4#) 35... Qh6+ 36. Kg3 Ne2+ {wins the queen.}) 32... Qe6+ 33. f5 Ne3+ 34. Kh3 Qxf5+ 35. g4 Qg5 {and white must give up the queen to avoid mate.}) 31. Qxd2 Qa3+ 32. Qe3 Qxe3+ $2 {After the exchange of queens, black's advantage evaporates, and it's white who can start playing for a win.} (32... Qb2 {maintains an advantage.}) 33. Kxe3 Ne7 34. Rb1 f6 35. Kd4 Nf5+ 36. Kc5 Ne3 $2 {Blundering a pawn in time-trouble.} (36... c6 37. Rb8+ Kf7 38. Bh3 {is much better for white, but black can still put up some sort of defence.}) 37. Bxd5+ Nxd5 38. Kxd5 Bf5 39. Rb2 Kf7 40. Kc6 {Diagram # This is now winning for white: the a-pawn can easily be stopped in its tracks; the c-pawn cannot.} Kg6 41. Kxc7 h5 42. Kb6 Be6 43. Rb5 a3 44. Ra5 a2 45. Kb5 Kf5 46. c4 g5 47. Rxa2 Kg4 48. fxg5 Kxg5 49. Rf2 f5 50. Rf4 1-0

