1. e4 Nc6 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 Bg4 5. Be2 (5. d5 $6 {is an attempt to punish Black's slightly unusual opening play;} Nb8 $1 {gives White no clear follow-up, though.} (5... Ne5 $2 6. Nxe5 $1 {is the point of the line -} Bxd1 7. Bb5+ c6 8. dxc6 dxe5 9. c7+ Qd7 10. Bxd7+ Kxd7 11. Kxd1 $14 {thanks to the mobile queenside majority.})) 5... Bxf3 6. Bxf3 e5 7. d5 Nd4 8. Be3 Nxf3+ (8... c5 {is probably better; after the text, White just has a nice space advantage with the better bishop.}) 9. Qxf3 Qd7 (9... Be7 10. O-O O-O 11. a4 a5 12. Qe2 Nd7 13. f3 f5 14. exf5 Rxf5 15. Ne4 h6 16. c4 b6 17. Bd2 Rf8 18. b4 axb4 19. Bxb4 Nf6 20. Nc3 Qd7 21. Nb5 Rfc8 22. Rfd1 Ra6 23. Ra3 Rca8 24. Qa2 {Berezjuk,S (2450)-Kriz,O (2255)/Slovakia 1997/0-1 (39)}) 10. Qe2 Be7 11. f3 {Diagram #} O-O $6 {This appears, from the subsequent course of the game, to be "castling into it".} (11... c6 {seems better, waiting for White to commit himself before Black does:} 12. Rd1 O-O 13. g4 Bd8 14. h4 Bb6 15. h5 Bxe3 16. Qxe3 Ne8 17. Ne2 cxd5 18. Rxd5 Nc7 19. Rd2 Qb5 20. Qb3 Qc5 21. g5 Rad8 22. g6 d5 23. gxh7+ Kxh7 24. Qxb7 Rb8 25. b4 Qc4 26. Qxa7 {Osadchenko,A (2180)-Prosviriakov,V (2335)/Moscow 1996/0-1 (40)}) 12. g4 c6 13. O-O-O c5 {The race is now on, as both sides fling their pawns at the enemy king, in true opposite-side-castling style.} 14. h4 Rfb8 15. h5 b5 16. g5 Ne8 {Diagram #} 17. g6 $1 {A typical sacrifice to open lines against the black king.} b4 (17... fxg6 18. hxg6 h6 19. Qh2 {with ideas of Bxh6 and/or Ne2-g3-f5 to come.}) 18. Nb1 Qb5 {Necessary if Black is to get ...c4 in, but it takes a defender away from the black king.} 19. Qg2 c4 20. h6 fxg6 21. hxg7 Nxg7 22. Rdg1 Qe8 (22... c3 23. Rxh7 Qe8 24. Rgh1 {leaves Black without a decent defence:} Kf7 25. R1h6 g5 26. Bxg5 $18) 23. Rh6 Kh8 24. Qh3 {Diagram #} Qg8 $2 (24... Nh5 25. Rgxg6 Qxg6 26. Rxg6 hxg6 27. Qe6 {is still very good for White, but Black might have some drawing chances in the endgame. The text leaves him permanently under fire.}) 25. Rhxg6 Bf8 26. Rh6 Rb7 27. f4 {To bring the knight in via d2 and f3.} Rf7 (27... c3 {attempts to cut across white's plan, but runs into problems of its own after} 28. f5 {, and f6 will win.}) 28. f5 Be7 29. Nd2 c3 30. Nf3 cxb2+ 31. Kb1 Rf6 (31... Raf8 32. Rgg6 {with Bg5 to follow.}) 32. Nh4 b3 33. axb3 1-0

