1. e4 e5 2. d4 exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 4. Qe3 Nf6 5. Nc3 Bb4 6. Bd2 O-O 7. O-O-O Re8 8. Qg3 Rxe4 9. a3 Bxc3 10. Bxc3 d6 {Up to this point, the game matches Smith-Ponter from round 2. Perhaps unsatisfied with the course of that game, Smith tries a different move here.} 11. f4 {Diagram #} Bd7 (11... g6 12. Be2 Bf5 (12... Re8 13. Bf3 Bf5 14. h4 Re3 15. Qg5 Ne4 16. Bxe4 Qxg5 17. hxg5 Rxe4 18. Nf3 Re2 19. Nd2 Bg4 20. Rh4 Rxg2 21. Rdh1 Bh5 22. Ne4 Kf8 23. Nf6 Ke7 24. Nxh5 gxh5 25. Rxh5 Re8 26. Rxh7 Kd8 27. Rxf7 {1-0 Payen,A (2373)-Glod,P (2213)/Bescanon 1999}) 13. Bf3 Rc4 14. Ne2 Ne4 15. Bxe4 Rxe4 16. Qf3 Qh4 17. h3 Rae8 18. Rhe1 {1/2-1/2 Ask,J (2260)-Moberg,K (2336)/Goteborg 2006}) 12. Bd3 Re8 13. Nh3 Nh5 14. Qf2 (14. Qf3 $2 {looks plausible, but} Qh4 {leaves white struggling to find a decent move.}) 14... Bg4 ({Fritz recommends} 14... Bxh3 15. gxh3 d5 {; it looks very risky to open the g-file for White, but it's not immediately clear how this can be exploited.}) 15. Rdf1 Qe7 $6 (15... d5 $13) 16. Re1 Qd7 17. Qh4 {Diagram #} g6 $2 (17... f5 {is better, although white still gets plenty of play against the black king:} 18. Rxe8+ Rxe8 19. Ng5 Nf6 20. Bxf6 (20. h3 $2 Be2) 20... gxf6 21. Bc4+ d5 22. Bxd5+ Qxd5 23. Qxh7+ Kf8 24. Qh8+ Ke7 25. Re1+ Ne5 26. Qg7+ $40) 18. Nf2 Bf5 (18... Rxe1+ 19. Rxe1 f5 20. h3 $18) 19. g4 Bxd3 20. gxh5 {Winning a piece; the double threat to d3 and f6 cannot be defended against.} d5 21. Nxd3 d4 22. Bd2 Qd5 23. Kb1 a5 24. h6 {The threat of mate ties Black's queen to f5, and thus enables White to swap all the major pieces off.} Qf5 25. Rxe8+ Rxe8 26. Re1 Rxe1+ 27. Qxe1 Qe6 28. Qxe6 fxe6 {Diagram # This ending is now easily winning for White.} 29. Nc5 Kf7 30. Nxb7 Kf6 31. Nxa5 Nxa5 32. Bxa5 c6 33. Bb6 e5 34. a4 exf4 35. Bxd4+ 1-0

