1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 d6 7. Bd3 e5 8. Ne2 Nc6 {Diagram #} 9. d5 {A somewhat committal move.} (9. f3 {is more flexible, waiting to see if Black will disturb the centre himself.}) 9... Ne7 (9... e4 10. Bb1 Ne5 11. Ng3 Qa5 12. Qb3 b6 13. Qb5+ Qxb5 14. cxb5 a6 15. bxa6 Bxa6 16. Nxe4 Nxe4 17. Bxe4 Nd3+ 18. Bxd3 Bxd3 19. f3 f5 20. Kf2 O-O 21. Rd1 Bc4 22. Bd2 Ra4 23. Be1 Rfa8 24. Kg1 {1/2-1/2 Van Elst,A (2283)-Rodriguez Cespedes,A (2482)/France 2003}) 10. Ng3 (10. e4 Ng6 11. O-O Nh5 12. Ra2 O-O 13. Bb1 Nhf4 14. Kh1 Nxe2 15. Qxe2 Qh4 16. g3 Qh3 17. f3 h5 18. Qg2 Qxg2+ 19. Kxg2 h4 20. Bg5 hxg3 21. hxg3 f6 22. Be3 Kf7 23. Rh1 Bd7 24. f4 Rh8 {Vennemann,J (1742)-Suess,H (1834)/Postbauer 2006/1/2-1/2 (40)}) 10... Bd7 11. f4 (11. O-O h5 12. f4 exf4 13. Rxf4 Ng4 14. Qf1 Ne5 15. Bb1 Qc7 16. Ra2 f6 17. Ne4 O-O-O 18. a4 Kb8 19. a5 Rdf8 20. Rff2 Ka8 21. Rfb2 h4 22. Rb6 Nc8 23. Rb3 h3 24. g3 Ne7 25. Ba3 f5 {Shevchenko,T (2204)-Biriukov,O (2377)/Pardubice 2005/0-1 (43)}) 11... Qa5 12. Bd2 h5 13. fxe5 dxe5 {Diagram # This is a position where appearances can be deceptive. At first sight, White may appear to have the advantage - he has the two bishops and a protected passed pawn. However, in practice, it will probably favour Black - he can easily stop the passed pawn by placing a knight on d6, from where it will menace c4 (and e4, if white advances his e-pawn; if not, it will support an ...f5 and ...e4 advance).} 14. h4 Qc7 15. e4 Ng4 {The last few moves have not been kind to White; Black will be able to follow up with ...Nc8-d6 and ...0-0-0, to be followed by ...g6 and ...f5, while White's plan remains obscure. In the game, it doesn't come to that.} 16. Nf5 $4 Nxf5 17. exf5 {Diagram #} e4 $1 18. Qe2 (18. Bxe4 Qg3+ 19. Ke2 Qf2+ 20. Kd3 Ne5+ 21. Kc2 Ba4+ {wins the queen.}) 18... Qg3+ 19. Kf1 Qxd3 0-1

