1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 b5 4. cxb5 a6 5. f3 e6 $5 {Definitely deciding to make a gambit of it;} (5... axb5 6. e4 Qa5+ 7. Bd2 b4 8. Na3 {is another playable line.}) 6. dxe6 fxe6 7. Bg5 (7. e3 d5 8. bxa6 Bd6 9. Bb5+ Nfd7 10. f4 Bxa6 11. Bxa6 Nxa6 12. Nf3 O-O 13. O-O Nb4 14. Nc3 c4 15. b3 cxb3 16. Qxb3 Qa5 17. a3 Nd3 18. Bd2 N7c5 19. Qc2 Qa6 20. Nd4 Rab8 21. Rfb1 e5 {Azmaiparashvili,Z (2610)-Benjamin,J (2530)/Reykjavik 1990/0-1 (59)}) (7. e4 d5 8. e5 Nfd7 9. f4 axb5 10. Bd3 g6 11. Qg4 Qb6 12. Be3 Bb7 13. Nf3 d4 14. Bf2 Na6 15. Bh4 Be7 16. Bxg6+ hxg6 17. Qxg6+ Kd8 18. Bxe7+ Kxe7 19. Qg7+ Kd8 20. Qxh8+ Kc7 21. Qh6 Nb4 {Kipp,L (1921)-Kortenbusch,C (2070)/Germany 2006/1-0 (36)}) 7... Be7 (7... axb5 8. e4 Qa5+ (8... c4 9. Nc3 Be7 10. Qd2 Ba6 11. e5 b4 12. exf6 bxc3 13. Qxc3 Bxf6 14. Bxf6 Qxf6 15. Qxf6 gxf6 16. Ne2 Nc6 17. Nf4 Kf7 18. O-O-O d5 19. g3 c3 20. Bxa6 Rxa6 21. Rhe1 Rxa2 22. Nxe6 cxb2+ {0-1 Berta,K (2225)-Szieberth,A (2390)/Hungary 1995}) 9. Bd2 Qb6 10. Be3 Nc6 11. Nc3 b4 12. Na4 Qa5 13. b3 Be7 14. Rc1 d6 15. Nh3 O-O 16. Be2 Kh8 17. O-O e5 18. Nf2 Nd4 19. Bc4 Bd7 20. Bxd4 cxd4 21. Qd3 Nh5 22. g3 {0-1 Birens,E (2140)-Vaisser,A (2565)/France 1996}) 8. Nc3 (8. e4 $44) 8... d5 9. e3 O-O {Diagram # It's interesting to note that the normally materialistic Fritz says Black has a clear advantage here, despite White's extra pawn. I can't say I disagree; it will be hard for White to develop in any meaningful way.} 10. bxa6 Nxa6 11. Bd3 Nb4 12. Bb1 $2 (12. Nge2 {has to be better - White can't afford to fall this far behind in development.}) 12... Ba6 13. a3 Nc6 14. Nh3 (14. Nge2 {no longer gives White time to castle -} d4 15. exd4 cxd4 16. Bxf6 Bxf6 17. Ne4 d3 18. N2c3 Bd4 {and White's position is falling to pieces.}) 14... d4 15. exd4 cxd4 16. Bxf6 Bxf6 17. Ne4 Be7 18. Nhf2 (18. Kf2 {, attempting to get the king out of the danger zone, is met by} Qb6 19. Kg1 Qxb2 {and black's regained his pawn to add to his other advantages.}) 18... Qa5+ 19. Nd2 (19. Qd2 Bb4 $19) 19... Ne5 20. b4 {Diagram #} Qb6 ({And not} 20... Bxb4 $4 21. axb4 Qxa1 22. Bxh7+ $1 {- White still has some tricks left.}) 21. Nde4 Nc4 22. Nd3 Ne3 {Having tried so hard to block the bishop's attack on f1, White now finds the castling-prevention duty has merely been transferred to the knight.} 23. Qd2 Bxd3 24. Qxd3 (24. Bxd3 Bxb4 $19) 24... Nxg2+ 25. Kd1 Nf4 $6 (25... Bxb4 {was probably better - both sides were getting short of time at this point.}) 26. Qb3 Rac8 27. Bd3 Qc6 28. b5 Qb6 {Diagram #} 29. Ra2 $2 (29. a4 {at least gi ves black something to think about - the text goes down without a fight.}) 29... Nd5 30. Be2 Qc7 $1 {The threat of mate on c1 is surprisingly hard to meet.} 31. Kd2 (31. Ra1 Ne3+ 32. Kd2 Qa5+ 33. Kd3 Nd5 $1 {traps white's king in a mating net:} 34. Rhe1 Nf4+ 35. Kxd4 Qb6+ 36. Ke5 Rf5#) 31... Qf4+ 32. Kd3 (32. Ke1 Bh4+ 33. Ng3 Rc3 $19) 32... Qe3# 0-1

